.TH INTUITION 4
.SH NAME
intuition \- draw server, compositor and window manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B intuition
[
.I keysource
]
.PP
.nf
.B /srv/draw/new
.B /srv/draw/ctl
.B /srv/draw/hotkey
.BI /srv/draw/ n /data
.BI /srv/draw/ n /ctl
.BI /srv/draw/ n /cons
.BI /srv/draw/ n /consctl
.BI /srv/draw/ n /mouse
.BI /srv/draw/ n /wctl
.BI /srv/draw/ n /cursor
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Intuition
posts its service as
.B draw
in
.B /srv
and serves the tree above on the other end.
It opens
.B /dev/fb
for writing, which diverts the screen away from the kernel's own console for
as long as the descriptor stays open, and
.B /dev/fbctl
to learn the screen's width, height and depth.
From then on
.I intuition
owns every pixel: it is the only compositor and the only window manager, and
what a window does not cover is desktop.
.PP
The optional argument names the file to read keys from, a message file
served by
.IR kbdfs (4);
with none given,
.I intuition
reads
.B /dev/cons
in raw mode instead, which is what the self-tests use.
.PP
Unlike
.IR rio ,
each window owns pixels of its own rather than a clipped share of one
screen image.
A client's commands land in its own window's memory, never on the glass
directly, and a window that becomes uncovered is repainted from what it
already held.
Nothing is ever told to redraw itself.
.SS Windows and sessions
There are room for thirty-two windows at once.
A window is a rectangle of memory bought from
.IR segattach (2)
when a client claims it and given back when the claim ends.
Reading
.B /srv/draw/new
answers the number of a window with no session, as a decimal string, or a
number past the last window when none is free.
The claim itself is an
.I lopen
of that window's
.BR data ,
described in
.IR open (5);
a client that loses the race to another finds the window already taken and
gets
.BR ENOSPC .
A window's directory is named by that number, so a client that has claimed
window
.I n
reaches its files under
.BI /srv/draw/ n .
.PP
A window is born with a frame around its client area: a bevelled border
and a title bar carrying four gadgets at its corners and the name last
written to
.BR ctl .
The close, depth, zoom and size gadgets are the pointer's; see below.
Three kinds of window wear no frame and answer to no gadget:
a backdrop, which sits at the back of the stack and never comes forward, a
bar, which stays at the top of the glass and is never covered, and a
popup, which is a menu or a list.
A
.B wctl
line makes a window one of these.
.PP
A session's data fid is write-only: the commands it carries are a stream
onto the window's own store, and a client reads its window back through
.B /dev/fb
and
.IR fb (3),
never through
.BR data .
Closing the data fid -- by a clunk or by a hang up -- ends the session, frees
the window's memory and every image the session allocated, and repaints
whatever the window had been covering.
.SS The data protocol
A session's
.B data
file carries the six-verb command stream
.IR draw (2)
encodes:
.BR alloc ,
.BR load ,
.BR fill ,
.BR blit ,
.BR free
and
.BR flush .
Nothing drawn reaches the glass until a
.B flush
command; before that, every store lands only in the window's own memory.
Image
.B 0
means the window's own client area in a
.B fill
or a
.B blit
destination, and a window can never be a source: reading pixels back is
.IR fb (3)'s
job.
A command past the edge of the client area, or one naming an image the
session never allocated, is clipped or refused; a malformed command fails
the write and leaves what came before it already drawn.
A session may also allocate images of its own, up to 2048 pixels each, out
of a pool of sixty-four shared by every window on the machine; these hold
a cursor, a glyph atlas, or anything else a client draws with before it
blits it into its window.
.SS The ctl and wctl files
A window's
.B ctl
answers its client area's size on a read, as
.IR fb (3)'s
own report:
.RI "width, height, the row's byte pitch, and the depth (always 32)."
A write is one of four lines:
.TP 8
.BI move " x y"
put the window at
.I x , y
on the screen
.TP
.BI size " w h"
make the client area
.I w
by
.I h ,
inside the frame the client is never told about
.TP
.B raise
bring the window to the front and give it the focus
.TP
.BI name " text"
set the words on the title bar, truncated at twenty-four characters, and
apply a
.IR workspaces (6)
rule if the new name matches one
.PD
.PP
A window's
.B wctl
is a superset of
.BR ctl :
.B move
,
.B size
and
.B raise
are the same three lines, and a read answers a fuller report,
.IR rio 's
own line with a workspace number after it:
.RI "the window's rectangle on the screen (frame included), " current
or
.RI notcurrent , " " hidden
or
.IR visible ", and the workspace it is on."
The rest of what
.B wctl
accepts:
.TP 8
.B current
give the window the focus and bring it to the front
.TP
.B lower
send it to the back; the focus follows to whatever is now in front
.TP
.B zoom
fill the screen below the bar, or return to where it was before the
last zoom
.TP
.B hide
take the window off the glass without ending its session
.TP
.B unhide
put it back
.TP
.BI workspace " n"
move the window to workspace
.I n
.TP
.B backdrop
.TP
.B bar
.TP
.B popup
make the window one of the three unframed kinds
.PD
.PP
A
.B close
line is refused: there is no way to ask
.I intuition
to end a session it did not start.
A hang up of a window's
.B cons
-- what the close gadget does -- is the only way a session ends from
outside.
.PP
A window's
.B cons
and
.B consctl
are the console files
.IR cons (3)
describes, scoped to that window: a line typed at the keyboard while the
window has the focus is what
.B cons
reads, and
.B consctl
takes
.B rawon
and
.B rawoff
for the same two meanings.
.B mouse
is a queue of pointer lines in the window's own coordinates, in the same
forty-nine byte format
.IR mouse (3)
uses; a read parks until the pointer moves or a button changes while the
window has it.
.B cursor
takes the seventy-two byte cursor image Plan 9's
.B /dev/cursor
does, and the pointer wears it while it sits over that window; an empty
write goes back to the arrow.
.PP
.B /srv/draw/ctl
is
.I intuition's
own, not any window's.
Reading it answers the current workspace and how many there are.
Writing it takes
.BI workspace " n"
to switch, or
.B reload
to read the keys and workspace rules files again.
.B /srv/draw/hotkey
carries one line per read, the chords the server matched but did not act
on itself, verbatim from the keys file; see
.IR keys (6).
.SS The pointer
On a machine with a mouse,
.I intuition
reads
.B /dev/mouse
and moves a small cursor over the glass, drawn last after every repaint so
nothing under it has to know it is there.
A press decides what it landed on: the close, depth, zoom or size gadget,
the title bar, the bottom right corner, or the client area.
Anywhere else in a window raises it and gives it the focus.
A press on the bar begins a move and a press on the corner a resize,
either ending on release.
A machine with no mouse runs the same server with no pointer at all.
.SS Chords, gadgets and workspaces
A key held with alt, control or shift is a chord, matched against
.BR $home/lib/keys ,
or
.B /lib/keys
when there is none; see
.IR keys (6).
What
.I intuition
knows how to do itself it does -- close, lower, zoom, raise or move the
window in front, cycle the focus, switch or send a window to a
workspace, open the overview -- and everything else it does not
recognise is a line on
.BR hotkey .
.PP
A window carries a number from one to nine, its workspace; only the
current workspace's windows are on the glass.
.BR $home/lib/workspaces ,
or
.B /lib/workspaces
when there is none, names a workspace a window with a given name opens
on; see
.IR workspaces (6).
.PP
The overview chord dims the glass and tiles every workspace's windows at
once, three by three, scaled down from their own stores with no client
asked to redraw.
A click on a tile switches to that workspace, and a click on nothing, or
the chord again, returns to the one showing before.
.SH FILES
.TP 12
.B new
read it for a free window's number
.TP
.B ctl
the server's own workspace switch and reload
.TP
.B hotkey
the chords the server does not act on
.TP
.BI n /data
the command stream, write-only; opening it claims window
.I n
.TP
.BI n /ctl
the client area's geometry, and the four lines above
.TP
.BI n /cons
the window's console
.TP
.BI n /consctl
raw or cooked mode for
.IR cons
.TP
.BI n /mouse
the pointer over the window
.TP
.BI n /wctl
the fuller geometry and the ten window manager lines
.TP
.BI n /cursor
the pointer's image while it is over the window
.SH SOURCE
.B servers/intuition
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR draw (2),
.IR chrome (2),
.IR mui (2),
.IR keys (6),
.IR workspaces (6),
.IR window (1),
.IR fb (3),
.IR mouse (3),
.IR kbdfs (4),
.IR cons (3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A claim on a window already taken, or a request when none is free,
fails with
.BR ENOSPC .
A malformed command or ctl line fails with
.BR EINVAL .
A write to a read-only file, such as
.B mouse
or a session's own
.BR data ,
fails with
.BR EPERM .
.SH BUGS
The image pool of sixty-four slots is shared by every window; a session
that allocates many images can starve every other window's client of
.BR ENOSPC .
There is no way to learn a window's name back once it is set; neither
.B ctl
nor
.B wctl
reports it.
